# Public demo config — the SINGLE source of truth for deploy/demo-oracle/deploy.sh.
# Copy to deploy/demo-oracle/.env (gitignored) and fill in. Never commit real values.
#
#   cp deploy/demo-oracle/.env.example deploy/demo-oracle/.env
#
# This file is authoritative for the deploy (file-wins) for durable config +
# secrets: a stale ambient var (a direnv-loaded .env.local, a leftover export in
# your shell) must not silently override what the public demo is running.
# EXCEPTION: the image-source vars are a per-run choice, so a caller
# (`just demo-oracle`) overrides them; the values here are the
# direct-deploy.sh default.
#
# Everything below is UNVERIFIED against a real Oracle box — see README.md.

# --- image source (defaults for a direct deploy.sh run; `just demo-oracle`
#     overrides per invocation) ---------------------------------------------
# There is NO local-build path in this mode, unlike `just local-helm local`.
# Two Ampere cores will not compile this workspace in a time anyone will wait
# for, and doing it on the demo box would starve the running demo of both cores
# while it happened. Deploy published multi-arch tags only; image.yml builds
# arm64 on native runners, so `edge` and `sha-*` already have an arm64 manifest.
IMAGE_REPOSITORY=ghcr.io/thulasi-ram/scarab-server
IMAGE_TAG=edge                            # or a published sha-<gitsha>
SCARAB_CLONE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/thulasi-ram/scarab-clone:edge
SCARAB_SIDECAR_IMAGE=ghcr.io/thulasi-ram/scarab-results-sidecar:edge
SCARAB_WSFETCH_IMAGE=ghcr.io/thulasi-ram/scarab-wsfetch:edge

# --- release ---------------------------------------------------------------
NAMESPACE=scarab
# The kube context deploy.sh is allowed to touch. k3s writes `default`; the
# guard exists for the same reason local-helm's does — so a deploy script can
# never be pointed at a cluster you did not mean.
KUBE_CONTEXT=default

# --- public identity -------------------------------------------------------
# The hostname the Cloudflare tunnel serves. It is used for THREE things and
# all three must agree, or each fails differently:
#   1. the run deep-link in every forge commit status,
#   2. the GitHub App's webhook URL   -> {SCARAB_PUBLIC_URL}/webhooks/github,
#   3. the OAuth callback            -> {SCARAB_PUBLIC_URL}/v1/auth/callback
# (3) is registered on the GitHub OAuth app; get it wrong and login fails at
# GitHub with a redirect_uri error that never reaches Scarab's logs at all.
SCARAB_PUBLIC_URL=https://demo-scarab.example.com

# --- Cloudflare Tunnel (ingress) -------------------------------------------
# Token of a remotely-managed tunnel, from the Zero Trust dashboard
# (Networks -> Tunnels -> your tunnel -> Install connector; the long base64
# blob after `--token`). deploy.sh puts it in the `scarab-cloudflared` Secret.
#
# The hostname -> origin route is NOT here: configure it on the tunnel's Public
# Hostname tab as
#   demo-scarab.<domain>  ->  http://scarab.scarab.svc.cluster.local:80
# See the header of cloudflared.yaml.
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=<tunnel-token>

# --- Cloudflare R2 (object store) ------------------------------------------
# The CAS: logs, artifacts, Depot packs, workspace snapshots. R2 rather than an
# in-cluster MinIO because Oracle can reclaim an idle Always Free instance —
# state that lives only on this box is state you will lose. The bucket is the
# durable copy of everything except Postgres, and the Postgres CronJob in
# postgres.yaml dumps into this same bucket nightly.
#
# Endpoint form is exactly `https://<account_id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com`
# (account id, NOT bucket name — the bucket goes in SCARAB_S3_BUCKET and the
# client addresses it path-style). Region is the literal string `auto`.
#
# ⚠ REQUIRED BUCKET SETTING (git-bug ad79c90): add a lifecycle rule that aborts
# incomplete multipart uploads after 1 day. The Depot streams drain bytes as
# multipart pack uploads and aborts its own on error and on graceful shutdown —
# but a CRASHED replica cannot, and S3-compatible stores give a server no way
# to list uploads it does not know about, so the leaked parts bill forever.
# R2 dashboard: bucket -> Settings -> Object lifecycle rules -> "Abort
# multipart uploads" -> 1 day. R2 does apply a DEFAULT 7-day rule, so this is
# tightening the window rather than filling a hole — MinIO expires them natively
# at 24h, and a demo bucket has no reason to carry a week of dead parts.
SCARAB_S3_BUCKET=demo-scarab
SCARAB_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account_id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
SCARAB_S3_REGION=auto
# An R2 API token scoped to THIS bucket with Object Read & Write. Not an
# account-wide token: the same credential is handed to the workspace service
# and to the backup CronJob.
SCARAB_S3_ACCESS_KEY=<r2-access-key-id>
SCARAB_S3_SECRET_KEY=<r2-secret-access-key>

# --- secrets (rendered into the chart Secret at deploy time) ---------------
# In-cluster Postgres (postgres.yaml). Neon's free tier cannot back this — 100
# compute-hours/month against an always-connected server is about four days.
SCARAB_DATABASE_URL=postgres://scarab:scarab@scarab-postgres.scarab.svc:5432/scarab
# base64 of exactly 32 bytes: `head -c 32 /dev/urandom | base64`.
# STABLE across redeploys, or every stored secret becomes undecryptable.
SCARAB_MASTER_KEY=<base64-32-byte-key>
# HMAC secret for /webhooks/github — must match the GitHub App's webhook config.
SCARAB_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<webhook-secret>
# Enables the results-egress sidecar + ingest (ADR-0042).
SCARAB_RESULTS_TOKEN_SECRET=<results-token-secret>
# Workspace service (ADR-0061). LEAVE THIS UNSET: deploy.sh generates one once
# into deploy/demo-oracle/.workspace-token-secret and reuses it, because the
# control plane mints tokens with it and the service verifies them — a value
# that rotated per deploy would invalidate every in-flight Step's credential
# mid-run and look exactly like the service being down.
# It MUST NOT equal SCARAB_RESULTS_TOKEN_SECRET (deploy.sh refuses if it does):
# the results token carries no verb and never expires, so sharing one would turn
# a results-write credential into a content read+write credential, and would let
# the workspace service forge step results for any {run, step, attempt}.
#SCARAB_WORKSPACE_TOKEN_SECRET=

# --- GitHub App (the forge integration, ADR-0046) --------------------------
# The App is what clones the repo and posts commit statuses. Its webhook URL is
# {SCARAB_PUBLIC_URL}/webhooks/github with the secret above.
SCARAB_GITHUB_APP_ID=<your-github-app-id>
# Absolute path ON THE DEMO BOX to the App's private key. deploy.sh puts it in
# a k8s Secret the chart mounts, so the server holds the credential AT BOOT and
# it survives a DB wipe (no POST /v1/secrets reseed).
SCARAB_APP_PEM=/home/ubuntu/secrets/scarab-demo.private-key.pem

# --- GitHub OAuth login (ADR-0049) -----------------------------------------
# REAL authentication. A separate thing from the App above: this is an OAuth
# App (or the App's OAuth credentials) whose Authorization callback URL is
# {SCARAB_PUBLIC_URL}/v1/auth/callback.
#
# Everyone who signs in lands as VIEWER — read-only, cannot dispatch runs, cannot
# write secrets. Only the logins listed in SCARAB_OAUTH_OWNERS get Owner.
SCARAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=<oauth-client-id>
# Never reaches helm values: deploy.sh puts it in the `scarab-oauth` Secret and
# the chart wires it in by reference (secrets.oauthClientSecret). The chart
# refuses to render an OAuth config with no secret it can reach.
SCARAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=<oauth-client-secret>
# Comma-separated. Matches the provider subject (GitHub `login`) or a verified
# email claim. Leave it empty and NOBODY can administer the install — including
# you. This is the maintainer's GitHub login.
SCARAB_OAUTH_OWNERS=thulasi-ram

# --- the two assumptions this mode is built on -----------------------------
# 1. the demo repo (thulasi-ram/scarab-ci) is PUBLIC, and
# 2. the demo is LOGIN-GATED (no anonymous access).
#
# Both are load-bearing, and together they are why it is safe to let any GitHub
# user sign in: a Viewer can read every run's logs and artifacts, and those
# contain the repo's source, its build output, and anything a step echoes. If
# the repo were private, "any GitHub user" would be a source leak. If the demo
# were open to anonymous readers, it would be a public one.
#
# Set this to `false` if either assumption stops holding, and deploy.sh will
# REFUSE rather than deploy. That is not a limitation being hidden: scarab-server
# has no sign-in allowlist today (SCARAB_OAUTH_OWNERS only ELEVATES a login to
# Owner; every other authenticated subject is admitted as Viewer), so there is
# no configuration that makes a private repo safe here. The fix would be a
# server feature, not an env var.
DEMO_ASSUME_PUBLIC_REPO=true
